# Codex rewrites machine-managed state (marketplace timestamps/revisions, hook
# trust hashes) into these files on nearly every launch. The `codex-config` clean
# filter normalizes that churn out of the committed blob so they stay stable
# across launches and machines. Install the filter with `just git-filters`
# (it is local git config, not carried by these attributes alone).
# Filter script: ~/scripts/mask-codex-state.sh
.codex/config.toml filter=codex-config
.codex/*.config.toml filter=codex-config

# `oc login` writes a live OpenComputer API key into ~/.oc/config.json
# alongside non-secret metadata. The `oc-config` clean filter strips the
# api_key field from the committed blob so the secret never enters git
# history; the on-disk file oc reads is never touched. Install the filter
# with `just git-filters`. Filter script: ~/scripts/mask-oc-config.sh
.oc/config.json filter=oc-config

# Pi rewrites cache-validator fields (checkedAt, etag, lastModified) into every
# provider object in ~/.pi/agent/models-store.json on nearly every model-list
# refresh. The `pi-models-store` clean filter normalizes those three fields out
# of the committed blob so real model data stays stable across refreshes and
# machines. Install the filter with `just git-filters`.
# Filter script: ~/scripts/mask-pi-models-store.sh
.pi/agent/models-store.json filter=pi-models-store

# OMP rewrites its config.yml files at runtime and the YAML emitter leaves a
# trailing space after keys introducing a nested block ("tools: "). That is the
# only rule it violates: across 12 committed versions yamllint reported
# trailing-spaces and nothing else, and prettier reported them all clean.
# Without this the churn lands in git, and a later `just format` adds a
# whitespace-only diff on top that buries real setting changes in review.
# OMP exposes no hook surface and a watcher would race its writer, so the
# normalization happens at the git boundary instead. Install the filter with
# `just git-filters`. Filter script: ~/scripts/normalize-yaml-ws.sh
.omp/agent/config.yml filter=yaml-normalize
.omp/profiles/*/agent/config.yml filter=yaml-normalize

# `trustedWorkspaces` in ~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json records a
# per-machine trust decision (absolute paths approved to bypass Antigravity's
# sandbox). The `antigravity-settings` clean filter strips that field from the
# committed blob so the trust grant is never replicated to another machine;
# the on-disk file Antigravity reads is never touched. Install the filter
# with `just git-filters`. Filter script: ~/scripts/mask-antigravity.sh
.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json filter=antigravity-settings

# ~/.claude.json accumulates Anthropic-managed state (oauth account, machine
# ID, verbatim first prompts, per-project session metrics) that gains new
# keys on most Claude Code releases and does not belong in git history. The
# `claude-json` clean filter keeps a strict allowlist -- top-level and
# per-project mcpServers plus MCP trust/context fields -- so the committed
# blob tracks only the hand-authored MCP registry. Install the filter with
# `just git-filters`. Filter script: ~/scripts/mask-claude-json.sh
#
# CHECKOUT HAZARD: smudge is `cat`, so `git checkout -- .claude.json` (or a
# stash pop, or a branch switch that touches this path) replaces the live
# file with the scrubbed blob, which logs Claude out and clears project
# trust. Recovery is re-running `claude` and re-authenticating.
.claude.json filter=claude-json
